            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for a cottage, then the Latin for a hut."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: arō, arāre",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "arō, arāre — \"to plough\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Vēre agricolae agrōs arant. In spring the farmers plough the fields."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "arō, arāre, arāvī, arātum. The plough is arātrum. English arable comes from it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "arō is inherited from Proto-Italic araō (EtymDB), with Greek aroō 'to plough' as a cousin (EtymDB; Lewis & Short); Lewis & Short add Gothic arjan. English arable comes from it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
