              "text": "vōmer, vōmeris — \"a ploughshare\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Vōmer ex ferrō est et terram dūram frangit. The ploughshare is made of iron and breaks the hard ground."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Third declension, masculine: vōmer, vōmeris m. (also vōmis). The iron blade of the plough."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The origin of vōmer is not settled: Lewis & Short give the sense but no source, and EtymDB lists it without a parent. English keeps vomer for a bone of the nose."
            }
          ]
        },
